    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d176">

                <pb facs="d175-1" n="1" sameAs="frus:frus1969-76ve10/pgd175-1"/>

                <head>176. Telegram 1730 From the Embassy in Costa Rica to the Department of
                        State<anchor n="1" xml:id="d176-f1"/>
                    <anchor n="2" xml:id="d176-f2"/>
                </head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName>San Jose</placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1970-07-06T23:00:00+00:00">July 6, 1970,
                            2300Z</date>
                    </dateline></opener>
                <pb facs="d176-1" n="1" xml:id="pgd176-1"/>
                <list subtype="subject" type="description">
                    <label>Subject:</label>
                    <item>Carlos Fonseca Amador</item>
                </list>
                <list subtype="subject" type="description">
                    <label>Ref:</label>
                    <item>San Jose 1621</item>
                </list>
                <p>1. President Figueres told me yesterday me yesterday GOCR still studying plan for Fonseca’s “escape” to third country.
                    Fonseca had turned down plan which included momentary asylum in Chilean Embassy
                    San Jose because he “would lose face” asking for asylum.</p>
                <p>2. Figueres indicated latest thinking was to deliver Fonseca directly to Cuba via
                    a secret LACSA flight. He might attempt arrange exchange for one of Cubans
                    imprisoned by Castro.</p>
                <p>3. If Cuba asylum could not be arranged, Chile, Bolivia, and Peru remained other
                    countries which might accept Fonseca. Spain remained a possibility, although
                    President indicated CR FonOff had not yet approached Spanish on this matter.</p>
                <p>4. Figueres promised keep Embassy informed developments.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed><hi rendition="#bold"
                            >Ploeser</hi></signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d176-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d176-f1-content">
                <p>Source: National Archives, RG 59, Central Files 1970–73, POL 29 COSTA RICA. Confidential; Priority. It was repeated to
                    Guatemala City, La Paz, Madrid, Lima, Managua, Santiago, San Salvador,
                    Tegucigalpa, and USCINCSO.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d176-f2" n="2" xml:id="d176-f2-content">
                <p>President Figueres told the Embassy that
                    the Costa Rican Government was still studying a plan to arrange the escape of
                    Sandinista captive Carlos Fonseca Amador to a third country.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
